 "next_text": "Tomorrow, we open another forgotten page of history, and we follow the people who lived it.",
 "text_sent": "Germanicus saved the man he later accused of killing him. According to Tacitus, a storm drove Piso towards a rocky coast. Germanicus sent warships to help. He was the emperor's adopted son, heading east to settle disputes among Rome's provinces and allied kingdoms. Piso, appointed governor of Syria, was supposed to serve under him. Instead, he gave soldiers gifts, replaced strict officers, and ignored orders to send troops to Armenia. The emperor's son held the higher command. Piso was building a following of his own. Then Germanicus fell ill near Antioch. Tacitus says human remains, spells, and lead tablets bearing his name were found hidden in the floor and walls. Germanicus believed Piso had poisoned him. Dying, he asked his friends to avenge him. After Germanicus died, Piso wanted Syria back. His own son urged him to return to Rome, warning that going back to Syria could mean civil war. Piso chose force. His men fought Roman troops. He lost. Now he had to face the Senate. Outside, crowds dragged off his statues to smash them and threatened violence if he escaped punishment. Inside, his accusers alleged poisoning. But Tacitus says they failed to prove it. He could be condemned for corrupting the army and taking up arms against Rome. That did not prove he had poisoned Germanicus. Before judgement, Piso was found dead in his room, a sword beside him. The Senate condemned him anyway, and ordered its decree displayed in bronze. Copies survive in Spain. They condemn his cruelty, his corruption of soldiers, and his attempt at civil war. They repeat Germanicus's accusation, but make no finding of murder. The man Germanicus saved was condemned as a rebel. <break time=\"0.5s\"/> Whether he had also been his killer remained unproved."
